---
description: "Workflow: Commit all changes and land them on main (worktree-aware, conversation-recording)"
globs: []
---
# Workflow: Task > Commit All to Main

<critical>You MUST use the correct commit prefix (feat, fix, task, docs, plan, chore).</critical>
<critical>You MUST auto-generate the message (do not prompt user).</critical>
<critical>You MUST embed the session's conversation in the commit body (Step 3). It is what makes a later merge resolvable.</critical>
<critical>You MUST write the message with `git commit -F <file>` — never `-m`. The body contains quotes, backticks and newlines that a shell-quoted `-m` will mangle or execute.</critical>
<critical>You MUST NOT push to origin unless a remote exists AND the user opts into the PR path.</critical>
<critical>You MUST NOT merge a branch that still has uncommitted changes.</critical>
<critical>If `main` has uncommitted changes, you MUST STOP and summarize — never merge over dirty work.</critical>
<critical>You MUST NOT remove a worktree while the shell is standing inside it.</critical>
<critical>This workflow may be invoked from ANY mode. Merging is not a product-code change, so the `core.mdc` PLAN guardrail does not apply.</critical>

<variables>
  {{repo_root}} - Main checkout path (`git worktree list` → the entry marked `[main]`)
  {{is_worktree}} - true if cwd is a linked worktree, not the main checkout
  {{branch}} - Current branch name
  {{main_clean}} - true if the main checkout has no uncommitted changes
  {{has_remote}} - true if `git remote` is non-empty
  {{convo_block}} - Timestamped conversation block harvested at Step 3 (may be empty)
  {{msg_file}} - Temp file holding the full commit message
  {{commit_hash}} - Short hash of the branch commit
  {{merge_hash}} - Short hash of the merge commit on main
</variables>

<workflow>

<step n="1" goal="Detect Context">
  <action>
    **Establish where we are**:
    - `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` → current tree
    - `git worktree list` → identify {{repo_root}} and set {{is_worktree}}
    - `git branch --show-current` → {{branch}}
    - `git remote` → {{has_remote}}
    - `git status --short` → current tree cleanliness
    - `git -C {{repo_root}} status --short` → {{main_clean}}
  </action>

  <check if="not in a git repository">
    <critical>STOP. Nothing to commit.</critical>
  </check>

  <check if="{{is_worktree}} is false AND {{branch}} is main">
    <action>Simple path: run Steps 2–5 only, then jump to Step 9. There is nothing to merge or clean up.</action>
  </check>

  <output>
    ✅ **Ending Step 1: Detect Context**
    📍 Worktree: [ yes | no ] · Branch: `{{branch}}` · Remote: [ yes | none ]
    🧹 Main checkout: [ clean | DIRTY ]

    ➡️ **Beginning Step 2: Stage & Analyze**
  </output>
</step>

<step n="2" goal="Stage & Analyze">
  <action>
    **Stage All**:
    - `git add -A`
    - If nothing to stage AND the branch has no unmerged commits, exit (nothing to do).
  </action>

  <action>
    **Analyze Changes**:
    - Identify modified/added/deleted files.
    - Determine primary type: `feat`, `fix`, `task`, `docs`, `plan`, `chore`.
    - `plans/` changes → `plan`. `docs/` → `docs`. `src`/`lib`/`scripts`/`tests` → `feat` or `fix`.
  </action>

  <output>
    ✅ **Ending Step 2: Stage & Analyze**
    📦 Staged all changes
    🔍 Determined commit type

    ➡️ **Beginning Step 3: Harvest the Conversation**
  </output>
</step>

<step n="3" goal="Harvest the Conversation">
  <action>
    **Why this step exists** — a commit records WHAT changed and WHEN it landed,
    never when the DECISION was made or what it superseded. Broz runs several
    sessions against one repo in an afternoon and closes them out of order, so
    the branch that merges LAST often carries the OLDEST instruction. His own
    timestamped prompts, in the commit body, are what makes a later conflict
    resolvable instead of a coin flip. See the `conversation-log` skill.
  </action>

  <action>
    **Harvest**:
    ```
    python3 ~/.slopdog/skills/conversation-log/scripts/harvest_prompts.py
    ```
    Run it from the tree being committed. It auto-detects repo, branch and time
    window (since this branch's newest commit, or its fork point if it has
    none), and captures EVERY session that worked this branch — not just the
    current one. stdout is the block; stderr is diagnostics.
    - Store stdout as {{convo_block}}.
  </action>

  <action>
    **Review before embedding** — read the block, do not just pipe it:
    - Does the window look right? Diagnostics on stderr print it. A window that
      opened days ago, or caught prompts from unrelated work, needs
      `--since <ISO8601>` to tighten it.
    - Anything truncated that mattered? Raise `--max-prompt-chars`.
  </action>

  <action>
    **Privacy gate** — the block travels with the commit EVERYWHERE the commit
    goes, and raw dictation is unedited:
    - `git remote -v`, then check whether the remote is public
      (`gh repo view --json visibility` when `gh` is available).
    - PUBLIC remote → STOP and ask Broz before embedding.
    - Private or no remote → proceed. This is the normal case.
  </action>

  <check if="the script errors, or returns zero prompts">
    <action>
      Do NOT block the commit. Set {{convo_block}} empty, say so in the report
      with a ⚠️, and continue. A commit without conversation is worse than one
      with it, but far better than no commit.
    </action>
  </check>

  <output>
    ✅ **Ending Step 3: Harvest the Conversation**
    💬 [N] prompts harvested · window [start → end]
    🔒 Remote: [ private | none | PUBLIC — asked ]

    ➡️ **Beginning Step 4: Generate Message**
  </output>
</step>

<step n="4" goal="Generate Message">
  <action>
    **Draft the message** in this exact order:

    ```
    <prefix>: <concise summary>

    <why/what — the reasoning that would otherwise be lost>

    Changes:
    - <file or group>: <what and why>

    {{convo_block}}
    ```

    - **Title**: `<prefix>: <concise summary>`, imperative, under ~72 chars.
    - **Body**: YOUR analysis of why — this is not made redundant by the
      conversation block. The block is what Broz ASKED FOR; the body is what was
      actually done and why. A reader needs both.
    - **Changes**: bulleted list of files/groups.
    - {{convo_block}} goes LAST, verbatim, including its `Session-Id:` trailers.
      Do not reformat, re-indent, summarize or reorder it — `merge_timeline.py`
      parses those markers back out.
  </action>

  <action>
    **Write it to a file** — this is why `-m` is banned:
    - Write the full message to {{msg_file}} (a temp path, e.g. under the
      session scratchpad).
    - Verify it round-trips: the file should still contain the
      `--- Conversation (` marker and the `[YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM UTC] broz:` lines.
  </action>

  <output>
    ✅ **Ending Step 4: Generate Message**
    ✍️ Drafted commit message ({{msg_file}})
    💬 Conversation block: [ embedded, N prompts | omitted — reason ]

    ➡️ **Beginning Step 5: Commit on Branch**
  </output>
</step>

<step n="5" goal="Commit on Branch">
  <action>
    **Execute Commit**:
    - `git commit -F {{msg_file}}` → {{commit_hash}}
  </action>

  <action>
    **Verify the record survived**:
    - `git log -1 --format=%B | head -40` — confirm the conversation block and
      the `Session-Id:` trailer are actually in the commit, not eaten by
      quoting. If they are missing, the commit is wrong: `git commit --amend -F`
      with the file rather than leaving a commit that lost its provenance.
    - `git status --short` must be empty.
  </action>

  <check if="tree still dirty">
    <critical>STOP. Do not proceed to merge with a dirty branch.</critical>
    <action>Report what remains unstaged and why.</action>
  </check>

  <check if="{{is_worktree}} is false AND {{branch}} is main">
    <action>Skip to Step 9. Work is already on main.</action>
  </check>

  <output>
    ✅ **Ending Step 5: Commit on Branch**
    💾 Committed `{{commit_hash}}` on `{{branch}}`
    💬 Conversation verified present in the commit body
    ✅ Branch tree clean

    ➡️ **Beginning Step 6: Main Safety Gate**
  </output>
</step>

<step n="6" goal="Main Safety Gate (HARD STOP)">
  <action>
    **Inspect the main checkout**:
    - `git -C {{repo_root}} status --short`
    - `git -C {{repo_root}} branch --show-current`
  </action>

  <check if="{{main_clean}} is false">
    <critical>STOP. Do not merge over uncommitted work.</critical>
    <action>
      Present to the user:
      - A summary of every uncommitted file in the main checkout, grouped by type
      - Whether it looks related or unrelated to {{branch}}
      - A concrete recommendation: commit it, stash it, or discard it
      Then WAIT for the user's decision. Do not proceed unprompted.
    </action>
  </check>

  <check if="main checkout is not on branch `main`">
    <critical>STOP.</critical>
    <action>Report which branch it is on and ask how to proceed.</action>
  </check>

  <output>
    ✅ **Ending Step 6: Main Safety Gate**
    🟢 Main checkout clean and on `main` — proceeding automatically

    ➡️ **Beginning Step 7: Land on Main**
  </output>
</step>

<step n="7" goal="Land on Main">
  <action>
    **Look before you merge** — if any other branch has landed on main since
    this one forked, check for overlap FIRST:
    ```
    python3 ~/.slopdog/skills/conversation-log/scripts/merge_timeline.py \
      --repo {{repo_root}} --ours main --theirs {{branch}}
    ```
    A clean-looking merge between two sessions that edited the same feature is
    exactly the case that silently reverts the newer decision. Cheap to check.
  </action>

  <action>
    **Choose the landing path**:
    - {{has_remote}} is false → **direct merge** (default; a PR is impossible without a remote)
    - {{has_remote}} is true AND `gh` is available → offer the user the PR path; if declined, direct merge
  </action>

  <action>
    **Direct merge**:
    - `git -C {{repo_root}} merge --no-ff {{branch}} -m "merge: {{branch}} → main"` → {{merge_hash}}
    - `--no-ff` is deliberate: it preserves the branch lineage so the session's work reads as one unit.
  </action>

  <action>
    **PR path (only if the user opts in)**:
    - `git push -u origin {{branch}}`
    - `gh pr create` with a title and body derived from the branch commits
    - Report the PR URL, then STOP. Merging the PR is the user's call.
  </action>

  <check if="merge conflicts">
    <critical>STOP. Do not attempt to auto-resolve.</critical>
    <action>
      Hand off to the merge workflow — it exists for exactly this:
      `~/.slopdog/rules/broz/workflows/task/resolve_merge.mdc` (`broz:resolvemerge`).
      It replays both sides' conversations in wall-clock order so precedence is
      read, not guessed. Report the conflicted files and leave the merge in
      progress.
    </action>
  </check>

  <output>
    ✅ **Ending Step 7: Land on Main**
    🔀 Merged `{{branch}}` → `main` as `{{merge_hash}}`

    ➡️ **Beginning Step 8: Return & Cleanup**
  </output>
</step>

<step n="8" goal="Return & Cleanup">
  <critical>Move the shell to {{repo_root}} BEFORE removing the worktree.</critical>

  <action>
    **Return to the main working directory**:
    - Change directory to {{repo_root}}.
    - If an `ExitWorktree` tool is available in the harness, prefer it — it moves the session, not just the shell.
  </action>

  <action>
    **Warn before destroying anything**:
    - Check the worktree for ignored-but-valuable files (`.env`, `.env.local`, local-only configs) via `git status --ignored --short`.
    - `git worktree remove` deletes ignored files too. If any are found, list them and ASK before removing.
  </action>

  <action>
    **Remove the worktree**:
    - `git -C {{repo_root}} worktree remove <worktree_path>`
    - If it refuses, report why rather than forcing.
  </action>

  <action>
    **Delete the merged branch**:
    - `git -C {{repo_root}} branch -d {{branch}}` — safe delete; fails if not fully merged.
    - Never use `-D`.
  </action>

  <output>
    ✅ **Ending Step 8: Return & Cleanup**
    📂 Returned to `{{repo_root}}`
    🧹 Worktree removed · Branch `{{branch}}` deleted

    ➡️ **Beginning Step 9: Summary**
  </output>
</step>

<step n="9" goal="Summary">
  <output>
    ### Commit Complete
    - **Branch commit**: `{{commit_hash}}` — [Title]
    - **Conversation recorded**: [ N prompts, HH:MM → HH:MM | none — reason ]
    - **Merge commit**: `{{merge_hash}}` (or `n/a`)
    - **Main is now at**: [short hash]
    - **Working directory**: `{{repo_root}}`
    - **Worktree**: [removed | kept — reason]
    - **Branch**: [deleted | kept — reason]
    - **Pushed**: no (no remote) | yes (PR #N)
    - **Status**: Clean
  </output>
</step>

</workflow>
